# Copy to remote/.env and fill in:  cp remote/.env.example remote/.env
# remote/.env is gitignored (.gitignore line 149). Never commit real values,
# and never put the client secret in THIS file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Auth0 ----
# The four values the API provider gave you in the curl --data payload, plus
# the tenant hostname the request goes to.

# Tenant hostname. Bare host or full URL both work.
AUTH0_TENANT=your-tenant.us.auth0.com

AUTH0_CLIENT_ID=
AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Which API the token is minted FOR. An Auth0 identifier, not necessarily a
# reachable URL - it is stamped into the token's "aud" claim and checked by the
# API server. A mismatch here is the usual cause of a 401.
AUTH0_AUDIENCE=

# From the payload. Only client_credentials is supported by these scripts.
AUTH0_GRANT_TYPE=client_credentials

# ------------------------------------------------------------- AtriumDB ----
# Where the AtriumDB API actually listens. This is a DIFFERENT value from
# AUTH0_AUDIENCE and has to come from the provider.
#
# Include the FULL prefix, version segment and all. The SDK appends bare
# endpoint names ("measures/", "patients/", "sdk/blocks") to this value, so a
# versioned API must carry the version here or every request 404s:
#
#   docs at https://host/api/docs  and routes like /v1/measures
#     ->  ATRIUMDB_API_URL=https://host/api/v1
#
# No trailing slash.
ATRIUMDB_API_URL=https://host/api/v1

# Optional. If set, test_connection.py uses it instead of minting a new token,
# which avoids an Auth0 call on every run while iterating:
#   export ATRIUMDB_API_TOKEN=$(python3 remote/auth0_token.py)
# ATRIUMDB_API_TOKEN=
